Read some time ago; I just happened to recall and jot down some reflections.
This book is more a collection of fragments about love from life than a collection of short stories.
The tone of this book is utterly bland, the kind of blandness found in plain water; it seeks simplicity in vocabulary and sentence construction, and the plot is also as quiet and uneventful as a street on a summer afternoon.
Every short story is a silhouette of a small segment in ordinary life, with no beginning and no end.
Finally, titled "What Do We Talk About When We Talk About Love?"
I believe that this collection of novels, along with its title, constitutes a form of performance art, with the author's intention being that love is not just the joy and sorrow of stage performances, the eternal sea and barren stones of urban legends, nor merely the entwined adoration of lovers. Love is not limited to these aspects either, and it should not be confined by them. Love also encompasses the mundane realities of life, the monotonous repetition of daily life, and the disorientation and avoidance of reality towards the future.
Reading this book, you may recall couples arguing beside you, boring marriages of people you know, and unhappy relationships.
This is precisely why I would not recommend it for aesthetic reasons; both its prose and plot are utterly bland, unless your life is stormy and tumultuous or as sweet as living in a Candy Castle, in which case you might find reading it merely routine and boring.
However, on a deeper level of thought, the everyday plot does not impress or expand one's ideas and perspectives (which differs from García Márquez’s Love in the Time of Cholera), and the brief segments do not explore any issues, such as what kind of mistake led to the failure of the romance.
What are the reasons for the breakdown of the protagonist's marriage?
So I wouldn't recommend reading this book; it doesn't really matter whether one reads it or not.
The author expresses more through technique and genre than through plot, which is also why the reading experience of this book is poorer.
