Strongly recommend Amazon to stop using EMS!
Product five stars, logistics one star, average three stars.
Don't understand why Amazon is sending EMS. Everyone in the industry knows it's unreliable, after all, computers are valuable items!
After placing the order, the delivery was expected to arrive on the 20th, so my family waited at home for three days!
Why does it take four to five days to send from Xiamen to Fuzhou?
This morning on the 22nd, I finally checked the logistics and it arrived in Fuzhou. I was preparing to happily receive it when, without any phone calls or messages, I checked the logistics later that evening and found that it had been signed for midday, with the note indicating it was signed by the recipient themselves!
I was instantly shocked!
Who am I? Where am I? Is this computer really mine?
Honestly, it's like questioning life, right?
Finally, I went to the pickup point to retrieve the computer.
The specific reason, in simple terms, is that the delivery boy mixed up the two tracking numbers, fortunately, the package didn't get lost.
I strongly suggest that Amazon don't use EMS anymore, and I definitely won't buy from them again if they start using EMS for their deliveries.
Awaiting supplier's response
The packaging has no labels. Simply call the supplier and tell them that the lack of labels means it's genuine. If there is any doubt, you can send a sample to Johnson & Johnson for inspection, leaving your license plate number and phone number as well. You promised to send me a message to notify me to enter my information onto the warranty card on their website. But I haven't received the message yet?
The packaging is damaged; time will reveal the quality of the product.
Severe damage to the outer packaging, while the inner box seems new. The manual appears not to be new; it was renovated in September and is stored in stock. I hope it won't be bad by the time it arrives. Could Amazon explain why several customers mentioned damaged packaging while you seem unaffected? Thank you!
